WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.229 --> 00:00:07.839
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:09.659
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:11.589
Minds, by Leonardo English.

00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:14.799
I'm Alastair Budge, and this is the third

00:00:14.869 --> 00:00:17.929
and final part of our series on Ireland:

00:00:18.269 --> 00:00:20.619
the nine centuries of struggle between

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:22.939
an island and its powerful neighbour.

00:00:23.499 --> 00:00:26.359
In part one, we followed Ireland from

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.399
the Norman arrival of 1169 to the

00:00:29.399 --> 00:00:32.429
Cromwellian settlement: how what began

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:35.019
as a temporary military favour became

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:37.469
a permanent occupation that stripped

00:00:37.499 --> 00:00:39.929
the Catholic majority of their land.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:42.889
In part two, we moved through the

00:00:42.889 --> 00:00:45.329
nineteenth century: the Act of Union,

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:47.629
the Great Famine that killed a million

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:50.409
people, and the brilliant, doomed career

00:00:50.429 --> 00:00:52.949
of Parnell, who came closer than anyone

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:55.559
before him to winning Home Rule — and was

00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:57.429
brought down before he could get there.

00:00:58.339 --> 00:01:00.829
In this episode, we follow Ireland

00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:04.889
from 1912 to independence in 1922.

00:01:05.569 --> 00:01:07.289
It is a story of rebellion,

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:09.889
guerrilla warfare, negotiation,

00:01:10.019 --> 00:01:11.939
partition, and civil war.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:14.719
And at the centre of it is

00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:16.199
one of the most remarkable

00:01:16.199 --> 00:01:18.369
figures in modern Irish history.

00:01:19.129 --> 00:01:20.639
Just in case you haven't listened

00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:23.039
to parts one and two yet, I will be

00:01:23.069 --> 00:01:25.509
picking things up from there, so if you

00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:27.709
haven't listened to those, now is the

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:30.229
time to press pause and go and do that.

00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:33.079
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:33.089 --> 00:01:35.866
minute, and get right into it.

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:39.739
So, let us pick up where we left off.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:42.739
The Home Rule Act became

00:01:42.739 --> 00:01:45.086
law in September 1914.

00:01:45.669 --> 00:01:48.089
It would create an Irish parliament

00:01:48.269 --> 00:01:50.839
for domestic affairs while Ireland

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:53.429
remained part of the United Kingdom.

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:56.699
For most Irish nationalists,

00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:58.439
it was less than they wanted.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:00.829
But it was progress.

00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:03.849
Ulster Protestants did

00:02:03.849 --> 00:02:04.959
not see it that way.

00:02:06.179 --> 00:02:08.249
You'll remember that Ulster, the

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:10.299
northernmost of Ireland's four

00:02:10.339 --> 00:02:12.839
provinces, had been home to Protestant

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:15.709
settlers since the early 1600s,

00:02:15.999 --> 00:02:18.299
following the Plantation of Ulster.

00:02:19.326 --> 00:02:22.146
Three centuries on, this community

00:02:22.366 --> 00:02:25.616
was British in identity, Protestant

00:02:25.806 --> 00:02:29.336
in faith, and deeply opposed to any

00:02:29.366 --> 00:02:31.846
arrangement that would put them under

00:02:31.846 --> 00:02:34.146
the rule of a Catholic majority.

00:02:34.786 --> 00:02:38.516
"Home Rule is Rome Rule" was their slogan.

00:02:40.156 --> 00:02:43.886
In September 1912, nearly half a million

00:02:44.176 --> 00:02:46.476
Ulster Protestants signed something

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.596
called the Ulster Covenant, pledging to

00:02:49.596 --> 00:02:53.346
resist Home Rule by all means necessary.

00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:55.656
And not only this.

00:02:56.056 --> 00:02:59.006
The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed: an

00:02:59.066 --> 00:03:02.086
armed militia of around a hundred thousand

00:03:02.106 --> 00:03:06.206
men, led by Edward Carson, a Dublin-born

00:03:06.236 --> 00:03:08.226
barrister who had become the most

00:03:08.256 --> 00:03:11.206
prominent voice of Irish unionism, the

00:03:11.206 --> 00:03:13.866
belief that Ireland, or at least Ulster,

00:03:14.186 --> 00:03:16.576
should remain part of the United Kingdom.

00:03:17.836 --> 00:03:21.676
In April 1914, the UVF had brought

00:03:21.676 --> 00:03:24.856
ashore twenty-five thousand rifles at

00:03:24.856 --> 00:03:27.706
Larne in County Antrim, in the dead

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:30.056
of night, without a single arrest,

00:03:30.346 --> 00:03:32.676
showing you quite how institutionally

00:03:32.676 --> 00:03:34.616
tolerated this movement was.

00:03:35.566 --> 00:03:38.482
In response, Irish Nationalists

00:03:38.646 --> 00:03:40.546
had formed their own armed

00:03:40.626 --> 00:03:43.426
organisation, the Irish Volunteers.

00:03:44.846 --> 00:03:47.736
Ireland now had two large private

00:03:47.746 --> 00:03:50.226
militias on opposite sides.

00:03:51.506 --> 00:03:54.294
And then, in August 1914,

00:03:54.726 --> 00:03:56.506
the First World War began.

00:03:57.556 --> 00:03:59.406
The Home Rule Act was

00:03:59.456 --> 00:04:01.086
immediately suspended.

00:04:01.766 --> 00:04:04.516
John Redmond, the leader of the Irish

00:04:04.536 --> 00:04:06.556
Parliamentary Party, agreed to the

00:04:06.556 --> 00:04:08.956
delay, confident the war would be

00:04:08.956 --> 00:04:11.286
short and Home Rule would follow.

00:04:12.306 --> 00:04:13.446
It would be a decision

00:04:13.666 --> 00:04:15.396
with enormous consequences.

00:04:16.236 --> 00:04:19.086
For moderate Irish nationalists,

00:04:19.406 --> 00:04:21.096
this was of course frustrating.

00:04:21.716 --> 00:04:24.206
But for the more radical wing

00:04:24.216 --> 00:04:26.166
of the movement, those who had

00:04:26.166 --> 00:04:28.046
never believed Britain would

00:04:28.046 --> 00:04:29.986
willingly give Ireland meaningful

00:04:29.996 --> 00:04:32.306
self-government, it was confirmation

00:04:32.576 --> 00:04:35.056
of everything they had always argued.

00:04:36.446 --> 00:04:39.526
And they began to plan accordingly.

00:04:40.296 --> 00:04:43.006
Now, most Irish political leaders

00:04:43.306 --> 00:04:45.366
supported Irish participation

00:04:45.616 --> 00:04:46.846
in the First World War.

00:04:47.786 --> 00:04:50.326
Around two hundred thousand Irishmen

00:04:50.326 --> 00:04:53.666
eventually served, from both sides of the

00:04:53.666 --> 00:04:56.396
political and religious divide, in British

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:59.076
uniform, fighting in France and Belgium.

00:05:00.406 --> 00:05:04.016
But a small group saw things differently.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:07.306
While their countrymen were dying

00:05:07.316 --> 00:05:10.116
in the trenches, they saw Britain's

00:05:10.146 --> 00:05:12.956
difficulty as Ireland's opportunity.

00:05:14.226 --> 00:05:15.916
And they began to move.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:19.466
The Irish Republican Brotherhood, the

00:05:19.676 --> 00:05:21.866
secret republican organisation we met

00:05:21.876 --> 00:05:24.276
in part two, which had always believed

00:05:24.366 --> 00:05:27.456
only force could win independence, they

00:05:27.676 --> 00:05:30.106
had been quietly preparing an uprising.

00:05:31.396 --> 00:05:34.406
Their intellectual leader was a man named

00:05:34.486 --> 00:05:37.386
Patrick Pearse, a schoolteacher and poet

00:05:37.686 --> 00:05:40.196
who believed, with an almost religious

00:05:40.226 --> 00:05:42.796
conviction, that Ireland required a

00:05:42.806 --> 00:05:46.046
blood sacrifice to be reborn as a nation.

00:05:46.876 --> 00:05:48.726
He knew from the beginning that

00:05:48.726 --> 00:05:50.846
it would probably fail militarily.

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:54.186
He believed it only needed to inspire.

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:56.856
Also at the centre of the planning

00:05:57.106 --> 00:05:59.526
was James Connolly, a trade

00:05:59.536 --> 00:06:01.876
union leader and socialist with a

00:06:01.876 --> 00:06:03.856
small private militia of his own.

00:06:04.486 --> 00:06:07.466
And Tom Clarke, a tobacconist in

00:06:07.466 --> 00:06:09.426
his late fifties, who had already

00:06:09.426 --> 00:06:11.206
spent fifteen years in British

00:06:11.236 --> 00:06:13.536
prisons for republican activities.

00:06:14.246 --> 00:06:16.676
He was the oldest of the conspirators,

00:06:17.046 --> 00:06:19.966
and regarded himself as the keeper of a

00:06:19.996 --> 00:06:22.636
tradition stretching back to the Fenians.

00:06:23.886 --> 00:06:26.856
The plan went wrong almost immediately.

00:06:28.016 --> 00:06:29.906
They had sought help from Germany,

00:06:30.146 --> 00:06:31.666
which was perfectly willing to

00:06:31.666 --> 00:06:34.446
oblige; as destabilising Ireland

00:06:34.446 --> 00:06:36.306
and tying down British troops

00:06:36.556 --> 00:06:38.606
served German interests quite well.

00:06:39.436 --> 00:06:42.806
The Germans agreed to send weapons, and

00:06:42.996 --> 00:06:45.746
one of the conspirators sent to manage

00:06:45.746 --> 00:06:48.226
the arrangement was Roger Casement.

00:06:49.716 --> 00:06:52.906
Now, he was a fascinating man; he was

00:06:52.906 --> 00:06:55.436
a former British diplomat who had been

00:06:55.446 --> 00:06:57.926
knighted for his work exposing human

00:06:57.926 --> 00:07:00.616
rights abuses in the Congo, and who

00:07:00.616 --> 00:07:03.156
had since turned to Irish nationalism.

00:07:04.286 --> 00:07:06.936
He returned to Ireland by German

00:07:06.946 --> 00:07:09.376
submarine, was arrested on the

00:07:09.376 --> 00:07:11.556
coast before the Rising could begin,

00:07:12.016 --> 00:07:14.296
and was later hanged in London.

00:07:15.366 --> 00:07:18.436
Then, a German ship carrying twenty

00:07:18.436 --> 00:07:20.836
thousand rifles was intercepted

00:07:21.066 --> 00:07:23.366
by the Royal Navy and sunk.

00:07:24.636 --> 00:07:27.106
Eoin MacNeill, the head of the Irish

00:07:27.116 --> 00:07:29.366
Volunteers, who had only been told of

00:07:29.366 --> 00:07:31.976
the plan at the last minute, issued

00:07:31.996 --> 00:07:34.636
a public order cancelling the planned

00:07:34.656 --> 00:07:36.676
mobilisation for Easter Sunday.

00:07:37.706 --> 00:07:40.256
The Military Council went ahead anyway.

00:07:41.596 --> 00:07:44.246
On Easter Monday, the twenty-fourth

00:07:44.246 --> 00:07:49.006
of April 1916, around 1,600 volunteers

00:07:49.356 --> 00:07:51.966
seized key positions across Dublin.

00:07:52.896 --> 00:07:55.556
The General Post Office on O'Connell

00:07:55.556 --> 00:07:57.746
Street, Dublin's main thoroughfare,

00:07:58.222 --> 00:08:00.239
became the headquarters of the rebellion.

00:08:01.089 --> 00:08:03.499
Patrick Pearse stood outside it

00:08:03.739 --> 00:08:06.209
and read aloud the Proclamation

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:07.749
of the Irish Republic.

00:08:08.679 --> 00:08:11.049
"We declare the right of the people

00:08:11.049 --> 00:08:13.009
of Ireland to the ownership of

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:14.839
Ireland," the proclamation said.

00:08:16.049 --> 00:08:19.059
It was signed by seven men — the members

00:08:19.219 --> 00:08:21.379
of the Military Council, who had planned

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:24.449
the rising in secret and who knew, as they

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:26.859
signed, that they were in all likelihood

00:08:27.099 --> 00:08:29.149
signing their own death warrants.

00:08:30.559 --> 00:08:33.239
The British response was swift.

00:08:34.129 --> 00:08:35.979
Around twelve thousand soldiers

00:08:36.139 --> 00:08:39.139
were deployed to Dublin within days.

00:08:39.839 --> 00:08:42.889
A gunboat on the River Liffey, the

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:44.369
river running through the heart

00:08:44.369 --> 00:08:46.609
of the city, shelled the centre.

00:08:47.229 --> 00:08:48.629
Buildings burned.

00:08:49.049 --> 00:08:51.619
The fighting lasted six days.

00:08:52.619 --> 00:08:56.379
Around 450 people died, more than half of

00:08:56.379 --> 00:08:58.799
them civilians caught in the crossfire.

00:08:59.769 --> 00:09:01.559
The rebels surrendered on

00:09:01.559 --> 00:09:02.869
the twenty-ninth of April.

00:09:04.079 --> 00:09:07.259
And public reaction in Dublin was

00:09:07.259 --> 00:09:09.649
not sympathetic to the rebels' cause.

00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:12.459
Many Dubliners were angry.

00:09:12.969 --> 00:09:14.469
The city had been damaged.

00:09:14.859 --> 00:09:18.209
With so many Irish families having sons,

00:09:18.289 --> 00:09:20.759
brothers and husbands in France, the

00:09:20.759 --> 00:09:24.159
Rising felt, to many, like a betrayal.

00:09:25.239 --> 00:09:26.949
When the rebel leaders were

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:28.369
marched through the streets

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:30.849
after their surrender, some were

00:09:30.919 --> 00:09:33.809
jeered, shouted at by passers-by.

00:09:34.849 --> 00:09:36.629
Then the British government

00:09:36.789 --> 00:09:39.509
made a terrible miscalculation.

00:09:40.759 --> 00:09:43.629
Fourteen rebel leaders were executed in

00:09:43.629 --> 00:09:47.129
Dublin over ten days in May, one or two

00:09:47.129 --> 00:09:50.639
at a time, so that each execution arrived

00:09:50.659 --> 00:09:53.209
in the newspapers as a separate shock.

00:09:54.029 --> 00:09:56.289
A fifteenth, Thomas Kent,

00:09:56.429 --> 00:09:57.919
was executed in Cork.

00:09:58.199 --> 00:10:00.799
And a sixteenth, Roger Casement,

00:10:01.089 --> 00:10:03.469
was hanged in London in August.

00:10:04.569 --> 00:10:07.069
James Connolly had been so badly

00:10:07.079 --> 00:10:08.499
wounded during the fighting

00:10:08.889 --> 00:10:10.069
that he could not stand.

00:10:10.659 --> 00:10:13.829
He was tied to a chair and shot.

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:17.579
This caused a shift in public opinion.

00:10:18.529 --> 00:10:20.459
The men who had been jeered as

00:10:20.459 --> 00:10:23.079
troublemakers became, one by

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:25.069
one, as their executions were

00:10:25.069 --> 00:10:27.369
announced in the press, martyrs.

00:10:28.309 --> 00:10:30.459
The Rising, which had been a complete

00:10:30.469 --> 00:10:32.809
military failure, had become the

00:10:32.819 --> 00:10:34.929
founding myth of a revolution.

00:10:35.959 --> 00:10:38.299
The British government had handed its

00:10:38.299 --> 00:10:41.099
opponents exactly what they needed.

00:10:42.289 --> 00:10:44.089
In the two years that followed,

00:10:44.439 --> 00:10:46.669
Irish politics was transformed.

00:10:47.959 --> 00:10:51.109
Sinn Féin, a small nationalist party

00:10:51.381 --> 00:10:54.449
that had existed since 1905, and which

00:10:54.449 --> 00:10:56.859
the British press had wrongly blamed for

00:10:56.859 --> 00:11:00.009
the Uprising, it grew rapidly to become

00:11:00.009 --> 00:11:02.539
the dominant voice of Irish nationalism.

00:11:03.419 --> 00:11:06.049
The old Irish Parliamentary Party, which

00:11:06.049 --> 00:11:08.519
was associated with the failed policy of

00:11:08.519 --> 00:11:11.379
waiting for Home Rule, well, it collapsed.

00:11:12.226 --> 00:11:15.617
And in December 1918, the United

00:11:15.617 --> 00:11:17.737
Kingdom held a general election.

00:11:19.067 --> 00:11:22.597
Sinn Féin won seventy-three of Ireland's

00:11:22.597 --> 00:11:24.897
hundred and five Westminster seats.

00:11:26.107 --> 00:11:28.007
But they had no intention

00:11:28.007 --> 00:11:29.427
of going to Westminster.

00:11:30.157 --> 00:11:32.447
They had campaigned on a policy of

00:11:32.507 --> 00:11:35.367
abstentionism: refusing to sit in the

00:11:35.377 --> 00:11:37.767
British Parliament even if elected.

00:11:39.077 --> 00:11:41.767
Instead, on the twenty-first of January

00:11:41.947 --> 00:11:45.117
1919, the elected members assembled

00:11:45.117 --> 00:11:47.557
in Dublin as the First Dáil Éireann,

00:11:47.857 --> 00:11:50.077
meaning the Assembly of Ireland, and

00:11:50.077 --> 00:11:52.117
declared themselves the parliament

00:11:52.277 --> 00:11:54.917
of an independent Irish Republic.

00:11:55.867 --> 00:11:59.797
On that same day, in County Tipperary, two

00:11:59.797 --> 00:12:02.397
members of the Royal Irish Constabulary,

00:12:02.677 --> 00:12:04.627
the British-controlled police force in

00:12:04.627 --> 00:12:07.517
Ireland, were ambushed and shot dead.

00:12:08.357 --> 00:12:11.137
The War of Independence had begun.

00:12:12.507 --> 00:12:14.937
Now, the War of Independence

00:12:15.147 --> 00:12:17.687
was not a conventional conflict.

00:12:18.137 --> 00:12:19.497
There were no front

00:12:19.507 --> 00:12:21.587
lines, no pitched battles.

00:12:21.887 --> 00:12:24.487
It was a guerrilla campaign, with the

00:12:24.727 --> 00:12:27.327
Irish Republican Army, as the Irish

00:12:27.337 --> 00:12:29.773
Volunteers had now become, carrying out

00:12:30.103 --> 00:12:32.883
ambushes and assassinations, and then

00:12:33.143 --> 00:12:35.833
melting back into the civilian population.

00:12:36.673 --> 00:12:39.193
In towns and villages across Ireland,

00:12:39.673 --> 00:12:41.563
police barracks were attacked,

00:12:41.823 --> 00:12:44.303
patrols were ambushed on country

00:12:44.303 --> 00:12:47.353
roads, and local officials seen as

00:12:47.463 --> 00:12:50.123
agents of British rule were targeted.

00:12:51.143 --> 00:12:53.403
The British responded with raids,

00:12:53.643 --> 00:12:56.253
arrests, burnings, and reprisals

00:12:56.433 --> 00:12:57.993
against civilian communities.

00:12:58.683 --> 00:13:01.543
It was a dirty, intimate war,

00:13:01.883 --> 00:13:03.983
fought street by street in some

00:13:03.983 --> 00:13:06.973
places and hedge by hedge in others.

00:13:08.153 --> 00:13:10.653
And no single person shaped that

00:13:10.653 --> 00:13:13.043
campaign more than Michael Collins.

00:13:13.973 --> 00:13:16.073
Collins was from West Cork,

00:13:16.193 --> 00:13:17.843
in the south of the country.

00:13:18.383 --> 00:13:20.473
He had worked in London as a young

00:13:20.473 --> 00:13:23.373
man, returned to Ireland for the Easter

00:13:23.373 --> 00:13:26.213
Rising, and was interned in Wales

00:13:26.253 --> 00:13:28.643
afterwards with hundreds of other rebels.

00:13:29.193 --> 00:13:31.623
He was twenty-five years old at the time.

00:13:33.043 --> 00:13:35.113
The months in the internment camp

00:13:35.433 --> 00:13:38.843
gave Collins time, contacts, and

00:13:38.993 --> 00:13:41.303
a very clear idea of what the

00:13:41.303 --> 00:13:43.363
movement needed to do differently.

00:13:43.963 --> 00:13:46.053
He had seen a rising fail.

00:13:46.583 --> 00:13:48.023
He was not going to repeat it.

00:13:49.381 --> 00:13:52.371
His role in the War of Independence was

00:13:52.371 --> 00:13:54.851
not that of a battlefield commander.

00:13:55.411 --> 00:13:58.641
He was an organiser, a spymaster,

00:13:58.991 --> 00:14:01.171
and a strategist, which made him,

00:14:01.181 --> 00:14:03.511
in some ways, even more dangerous.

00:14:04.551 --> 00:14:07.711
He built an intelligence network inside

00:14:07.721 --> 00:14:09.601
the British administration in Dublin.

00:14:10.081 --> 00:14:12.641
He had sources in the post office, in

00:14:12.641 --> 00:14:15.111
the police, among domestic staff in

00:14:15.111 --> 00:14:17.571
the homes of senior British officials.

00:14:18.221 --> 00:14:20.411
He knew, often within hours,

00:14:20.641 --> 00:14:22.021
what the British were planning.

00:14:22.628 --> 00:14:24.788
He used that intelligence to

00:14:24.808 --> 00:14:27.548
systematically dismantle the British

00:14:27.568 --> 00:14:29.188
intelligence operation in Ireland,

00:14:29.718 --> 00:14:32.058
targeting the detectives and officers

00:14:32.338 --> 00:14:34.759
gathering information on the IRA.

00:14:35.828 --> 00:14:38.158
Meanwhile, Collins moved around

00:14:38.168 --> 00:14:41.218
Dublin openly, on a bicycle, going

00:14:41.218 --> 00:14:43.208
to meetings and social events,

00:14:43.458 --> 00:14:45.908
known by sight to half the city.

00:14:46.748 --> 00:14:48.888
For three years, the British

00:14:48.918 --> 00:14:50.938
authorities who desperately wanted

00:14:50.938 --> 00:14:53.008
to capture him could not find him.

00:14:54.408 --> 00:14:57.588
And by 1920, the Royal Irish

00:14:57.618 --> 00:15:00.218
Constabulary was demoralised and

00:15:00.238 --> 00:15:02.098
barely functional in many areas.

00:15:02.848 --> 00:15:05.148
The British government responded by

00:15:05.188 --> 00:15:07.658
recruiting thousands of former First World

00:15:07.658 --> 00:15:10.058
War soldiers as temporary constables.

00:15:11.028 --> 00:15:13.148
There were not enough proper uniforms,

00:15:13.328 --> 00:15:16.188
so they were issued a mix of dark

00:15:16.238 --> 00:15:19.398
police uniforms and khaki army clothes.

00:15:20.278 --> 00:15:22.338
The combination gave them their

00:15:22.338 --> 00:15:25.028
nickname: the Black and Tans.

00:15:26.218 --> 00:15:29.248
A second force, the Auxiliaries, were

00:15:29.268 --> 00:15:32.118
former officers; they were better paid

00:15:32.308 --> 00:15:34.638
but considerably less disciplined.

00:15:35.828 --> 00:15:38.158
Neither improved the situation.

00:15:38.938 --> 00:15:42.238
Both became notorious for reprisals

00:15:42.258 --> 00:15:44.721
against civilian communities: villages

00:15:44.721 --> 00:15:48.101
burned, homes destroyed and people shot.

00:15:49.861 --> 00:15:52.041
The most concentrated single day of

00:15:52.051 --> 00:15:54.121
violence came on the twenty-first

00:15:54.121 --> 00:15:57.571
of November 1920: Bloody Sunday.

00:15:58.961 --> 00:16:00.991
In the early morning, Collins's

00:16:01.021 --> 00:16:04.021
men moved across Dublin and killed

00:16:04.241 --> 00:16:06.081
fourteen British intelligence

00:16:06.091 --> 00:16:08.731
officers in their homes and lodgings.

00:16:09.751 --> 00:16:12.301
That afternoon, a group of Auxiliaries

00:16:12.571 --> 00:16:15.761
drove to Croke Park, the main Gaelic

00:16:15.761 --> 00:16:18.551
football stadium in Dublin, where a crowd

00:16:18.581 --> 00:16:21.161
of thousands had gathered for a match.

00:16:21.831 --> 00:16:23.181
They opened fire on the

00:16:23.191 --> 00:16:24.671
pitch and the crowd.

00:16:25.511 --> 00:16:27.881
Fourteen people died, including

00:16:27.881 --> 00:16:29.411
a player on the field.

00:16:30.911 --> 00:16:33.251
Bloody Sunday did not end the war.

00:16:33.761 --> 00:16:36.131
But for Britain, it made the

00:16:36.151 --> 00:16:38.221
conflict impossible to ignore.

00:16:38.941 --> 00:16:40.531
The public was beginning

00:16:40.531 --> 00:16:42.291
to ask what exactly its

00:16:42.291 --> 00:16:44.041
government was doing in Ireland.

00:16:44.941 --> 00:16:47.041
And a truce was called on

00:16:47.041 --> 00:16:49.551
the eleventh of July 1921.

00:16:50.969 --> 00:16:53.411
Neither side had got what it wanted.

00:16:53.921 --> 00:16:56.701
Britain had not crushed the independence

00:16:56.701 --> 00:16:59.901
movement, but the IRA had not driven

00:16:59.911 --> 00:17:01.591
Britain out of Ireland either.

00:17:02.491 --> 00:17:05.991
So both sides now turned to negotiation.

00:17:07.441 --> 00:17:09.661
And the negotiations that followed

00:17:09.831 --> 00:17:11.911
were among the most consequential

00:17:12.091 --> 00:17:13.641
in modern Irish history.

00:17:14.961 --> 00:17:17.191
The Irish delegation was led by

00:17:17.261 --> 00:17:19.261
Arthur Griffith, the founder of

00:17:19.261 --> 00:17:21.551
Sinn Féin, and Michael Collins.

00:17:22.541 --> 00:17:24.951
Collins did not want to go to London.

00:17:25.561 --> 00:17:27.401
He understood the position he would

00:17:27.411 --> 00:17:30.071
be in: any agreement short of a

00:17:30.081 --> 00:17:32.971
full republic would be opposed by

00:17:32.981 --> 00:17:34.791
a significant part of the movement.

00:17:35.871 --> 00:17:37.691
He told a friend, as he left

00:17:37.691 --> 00:17:39.491
for London, that he was probably

00:17:39.501 --> 00:17:40.821
signing his death warrant.

00:17:41.421 --> 00:17:43.161
He did not mean it metaphorically.

00:17:44.311 --> 00:17:46.531
David Lloyd George, the British Prime

00:17:46.531 --> 00:17:48.661
Minister, and his negotiating team

00:17:49.161 --> 00:17:51.451
understood how to apply pressure.

00:17:52.321 --> 00:17:54.761
At the end of the talks, Lloyd George

00:17:54.941 --> 00:17:57.201
presented the Irish delegation with an

00:17:57.231 --> 00:18:01.101
ultimatum: sign the treaty as it stood,

00:18:01.441 --> 00:18:04.761
or face "immediate and terrible war."

00:18:05.901 --> 00:18:08.921
Griffith and Collins signed, in the early

00:18:08.921 --> 00:18:12.223
hours of the sixth of December 1921.

00:18:13.311 --> 00:18:16.371
The Anglo-Irish Treaty created the

00:18:16.451 --> 00:18:19.401
Irish Free State: self-governing,

00:18:19.621 --> 00:18:21.201
but within the British Empire,

00:18:21.641 --> 00:18:23.011
with the same constitutional

00:18:23.011 --> 00:18:25.501
position as Canada or Australia.

00:18:26.181 --> 00:18:27.941
Ireland would have its own

00:18:27.951 --> 00:18:29.251
government and parliament.

00:18:29.601 --> 00:18:32.001
But it would not be a republic.

00:18:32.601 --> 00:18:34.171
Members of the new parliament

00:18:34.371 --> 00:18:36.171
would have to swear an oath of

00:18:36.171 --> 00:18:38.221
allegiance to the British Crown.

00:18:39.581 --> 00:18:42.211
And Northern Ireland, the six

00:18:42.211 --> 00:18:44.481
counties in the north-east, was

00:18:44.481 --> 00:18:46.401
already a separate jurisdiction.

00:18:46.951 --> 00:18:49.901
A British Act of Parliament in 1920

00:18:50.251 --> 00:18:52.941
had created it as a distinct region,

00:18:53.211 --> 00:18:55.381
with its own parliament in Belfast.

00:18:56.101 --> 00:18:58.651
The treaty gave it the right to opt

00:18:58.721 --> 00:19:01.421
out of the new Free State, which it

00:19:01.441 --> 00:19:04.101
did, remaining in the United Kingdom.

00:19:05.161 --> 00:19:06.471
Collins's defence of the

00:19:06.471 --> 00:19:08.721
treaty was pragmatic.

00:19:09.111 --> 00:19:10.521
It was not the republic.

00:19:10.941 --> 00:19:11.501
He knew that.

00:19:11.881 --> 00:19:14.961
But it was, he said, "the freedom to

00:19:14.991 --> 00:19:17.951
achieve freedom." A stepping stone.

00:19:18.371 --> 00:19:20.501
You fight from where you are,

00:19:20.591 --> 00:19:22.481
not from where you wish you were.

00:19:23.481 --> 00:19:26.121
The Dáil voted sixty-four to

00:19:26.121 --> 00:19:28.045
fifty-seven to approve it.

00:19:28.431 --> 00:19:30.801
A margin of seven votes.

00:19:32.021 --> 00:19:34.071
The narrow vote split the

00:19:34.101 --> 00:19:35.821
independence movement in two.

00:19:36.611 --> 00:19:39.701
The anti-Treaty side, which included

00:19:39.731 --> 00:19:42.211
a significant portion of the IRA's

00:19:42.211 --> 00:19:44.921
fighting men, refused to accept

00:19:44.931 --> 00:19:47.171
the result and kept their weapons.

00:19:48.054 --> 00:19:49.801
Collins's new Provisional

00:19:49.801 --> 00:19:52.221
Government had to build an army

00:19:52.431 --> 00:19:54.761
while its opponents remained armed.

00:19:55.501 --> 00:19:57.881
For several months, both sides

00:19:58.011 --> 00:19:59.701
tried to avoid the worst.

00:20:00.591 --> 00:20:02.441
But, they could not.

00:20:03.421 --> 00:20:06.182
This led, in June 1922,

00:20:06.622 --> 00:20:08.431
to the Irish Civil War.

00:20:09.591 --> 00:20:12.511
Anti-Treaty forces occupied the Four

00:20:12.511 --> 00:20:15.021
Courts, the main courts complex in

00:20:15.021 --> 00:20:17.981
Dublin, as a statement of defiance

00:20:18.171 --> 00:20:20.181
against the new Provisional Government.

00:20:20.941 --> 00:20:23.241
Collins, who was now leading that

00:20:23.241 --> 00:20:25.791
government, gave the order to attack.

00:20:26.691 --> 00:20:29.081
Free State forces bombarded

00:20:29.081 --> 00:20:30.751
the building with artillery

00:20:30.971 --> 00:20:32.891
borrowed from the British Army.

00:20:33.671 --> 00:20:35.931
The irony was not lost on anyone.

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:38.940
The war that followed was

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:41.340
smaller in scale than the War of

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:44.270
Independence but more poisonous.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:47.710
These men had fought alongside each other.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:49.730
Some were friends.

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:51.020
Some were brothers.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.970
Around 1,500 people were

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.800
killed, including Collins.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:59.300
In the end, the government won.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:02.190
Anti-Treaty forces laid

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:05.069
down their arms in May 1923.

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:09.220
But the bitterness lasted for generations.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:13.260
And yet, in a strange way, Collins's

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:15.860
argument, that the treaty was a stepping

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.870
stone, was eventually proved right.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:22.910
The anti-Treaty leader, Éamon de

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:25.270
Valera, founded a new political

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:28.420
party and won the 1932 election.

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.900
He then spent the next fifteen years

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:35.170
dismantling the treaty piece by piece:

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:37.330
abolishing the oath of allegiance to

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.670
the Crown, reducing the role of the

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:41.960
British monarchy, and passing a new

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.820
constitution in 1937 that effectively

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:48.930
created a republic in all but name.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:53.300
And on Easter Monday 1949, the

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:55.230
thirty-third anniversary of the

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:58.920
Rising, the Irish Free State formally

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:00.940
became the Republic of Ireland.

00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:04.350
The choice of date was not an accident.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:08.230
So what does it all amount to?

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:11.140
Nine centuries of history, from

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:14.320
the Norman landing of 1169 to

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:17.400
the Civil War ceasefire of 1923.

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:20.190
In part one, we watched Ireland

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:21.970
absorbed by its more powerful

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:23.710
neighbour, step by step.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.750
In part two, we saw how Ireland

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:28.410
spent the nineteenth century

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.660
trying to escape that legacy.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:35.030
And in this episode, we followed the final

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:37.720
act: a small group of rebels who turned

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:40.790
a military failure into a revolution, a

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:43.530
guerrilla war that forced Britain to the

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:46.600
negotiating table, and an independence

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.820
that came at an enormous cost, and with

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:52.370
a wound built into it from the start.

00:22:53.783 --> 00:22:56.393
The decades that followed saw continued

00:22:56.393 --> 00:22:58.833
violence, especially in Northern Ireland,

00:22:59.183 --> 00:23:01.183
a conflict known as the Troubles,

00:23:01.403 --> 00:23:04.593
which lasted from the late 1960s until

00:23:04.593 --> 00:23:07.353
the Good Friday Agreement of 1998

00:23:07.723 --> 00:23:11.563
and claimed more than 3,500 lives.

00:23:12.223 --> 00:23:13.813
If you haven't listened to our episode

00:23:13.833 --> 00:23:16.863
on that, it's episode number 507.

00:23:18.203 --> 00:23:20.743
As for the relationship between Ireland

00:23:20.903 --> 00:23:23.403
and Britain today, it is no longer the

00:23:23.403 --> 00:23:25.523
relationship this series began with.

00:23:26.253 --> 00:23:28.353
Ireland is now an independent,

00:23:28.623 --> 00:23:30.081
wealthy European state.

00:23:30.613 --> 00:23:33.573
Britain is no longer an imperial power.

00:23:34.373 --> 00:23:36.453
And yet the two countries remain

00:23:36.493 --> 00:23:38.743
bound together by geography,

00:23:39.093 --> 00:23:41.083
trade, history, and people.

00:23:42.223 --> 00:23:44.513
Brexit was a reminder that the old

00:23:44.513 --> 00:23:47.083
questions never disappeared completely.

00:23:47.533 --> 00:23:50.243
The issue of the Irish border, which

00:23:50.253 --> 00:23:51.873
many had come to take for granted,

00:23:52.343 --> 00:23:54.313
suddenly returned to the centre

00:23:54.323 --> 00:23:55.993
of British and Irish politics.

00:23:56.523 --> 00:23:59.373
But for the most part, the relationship

00:23:59.393 --> 00:24:02.473
now is not one of domination or rebellion.

00:24:02.823 --> 00:24:05.323
It is one of two neighbours living

00:24:05.323 --> 00:24:07.553
with a history that neither can escape.

00:24:08.463 --> 00:24:10.823
What runs through all of it, perhaps,

00:24:11.193 --> 00:24:13.383
is a simple and enduring fact.

00:24:13.853 --> 00:24:15.343
You can suppress a

00:24:15.343 --> 00:24:17.093
people’s sense of identity.

00:24:17.203 --> 00:24:19.323
You can take their land, outlaw

00:24:19.343 --> 00:24:21.643
their language, ban their religion,

00:24:21.803 --> 00:24:24.133
and plant strangers on their soil.

00:24:24.553 --> 00:24:26.233
But you cannot make them

00:24:26.243 --> 00:24:27.533
forget who they are.

00:24:28.653 --> 00:24:30.863
OK, then, that is it for the third

00:24:30.923 --> 00:24:32.823
and final part of this mini-series.

00:24:33.033 --> 00:24:34.413
I hope it's been an interesting one,

00:24:34.463 --> 00:24:36.223
and that you've learnt something new.

00:24:36.693 --> 00:24:38.483
As always, I'd love to know what

00:24:38.503 --> 00:24:39.783
you thought of this episode.

00:24:40.173 --> 00:24:41.503
Have you been to Ireland?

00:24:41.723 --> 00:24:43.826
Are there parallels between the history of

00:24:43.873 --> 00:24:46.073
Ireland and the history of your country?

00:24:46.523 --> 00:24:48.373
I would love to know, so let's

00:24:48.373 --> 00:24:49.753
get this discussion started.

00:24:49.993 --> 00:24:51.083
You can head right into our

00:24:51.083 --> 00:24:52.723
community forum which is at

00:24:52.723 --> 00:24:55.263
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:24:55.263 --> 00:24:57.493
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:24:58.273 --> 00:25:00.073
You've been listening to English Learning

00:25:00.073 --> 00:25:02.663
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:03.013 --> 00:25:05.703
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:05.703 --> 00:25:07.933
I'll catch you in the next episode.

